From: deadbeef <deadbeef@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] tpm: qcom_qsee: support the dTPM backend

The Qualcomm qcom.tz.tpm QSEE application exists to expose a TPM, but it
does not always front an fTPM: on the Lenovo ThinkPad X13s (sc8280xp) the
SCM TPM info call reports QCOM_TPM_TYPE_DTPM (0x6454504d) while still
exposing the application and its control area normally.

The transport in this driver is backend-neutral. The control area is
discovered by querying the TA (QCOM_TPM_QSEE_CMD_QUERY_INFO_2) and the
command/response cycle, TZ memory bridging and TA kick are identical for
every backend; fTPM and dTPM differ in where the TPM is implemented, not
in the wire protocol. Only the probe-time type check was fTPM-specific.

Replace the inline fTPM-only test with qcom_tpm_qsee_backend_supported(),
which accepts fTPM and dTPM and rejects sTPM (not known to present the
same control area). dTPM is accepted by default so boards that report it
get a working TPM; `allow_dtpm=0` restores the old fTPM-only behaviour.
Log the selected backend at probe time so the active type is visible in
dmesg.

Tested on: Lenovo ThinkPad X13s (sc8280xp) reporting dTPM.

---
--- a/drivers/char/tpm/tpm_qcom_qsee.c
+++ b/drivers/char/tpm/tpm_qcom_qsee.c
@@ -33,6 +33,19 @@
 #define QCOM_TPM_QSEE_CMD_QUERY_INFO_2	(0x4000 | 18)
 #define QCOM_TPM_QSEE_CMD_SEND_COMMAND	(0x4000 | 8)
 
+/*
+ * Some Qualcomm platforms expose a dTPM rather than an fTPM behind the
+ * qcom.tz.tpm application. Both use the same QSEECOM control-area transport,
+ * so allow binding the dTPM backend. Default on: the transport is identical
+ * and refusing the backend leaves the TPM unusable on those boards.
+ *
+ * Set to 0 to restore the previous fTPM-only behaviour.
+ */
+static bool allow_dtpm = true;
+module_param(allow_dtpm, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(allow_dtpm,
+		 "Bind the dTPM backend via the qcom.tz.tpm transport (default: on)");
+
 struct qcom_tpm_qsee_control_area {
 	__le32 request;
 	__le32 status;
@@ -115,6 +128,32 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Backends this transport can drive.
+ *
+ * The transport itself is backend-neutral: the control area is discovered by
+ * asking the qcom.tz.tpm application (QCOM_TPM_QSEE_CMD_QUERY_INFO_2) and the
+ * command/response cycle is identical for every backend. fTPM and dTPM share
+ * that application and protocol; they differ in where the TPM implementation
+ * executes (TEE vs. a discrete/dedicated implementation behind the same TA),
+ * not in the wire format. sTPM is rejected: it is not known to present the
+ * same control area.
+ *
+ * dTPM is accepted unless qcom_tpm_qsee.allow_dtpm=0 is passed, so a board
+ * that reports dTPM gets a working TPM out of the box.
+ */
+static bool qcom_tpm_qsee_backend_supported(u64 type)
+{
+	switch (type) {
+	case QCOM_TPM_TYPE_FTPM:
+		return true;
+	case QCOM_TPM_TYPE_DTPM:
+		return allow_dtpm;
+	default:
+		return false;
+	}
+}
+
 static int qcom_tpm_qsee_app_send(struct qcom_tpm_qsee *qtpm, const void *req,
 				  size_t req_size, void *rsp, size_t rsp_size)
 {
@@ -339,12 +378,14 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to query TPM type\n");
 
-	if (tpm_type != QCOM_TPM_TYPE_FTPM) {
+	if (!qcom_tpm_qsee_backend_supported(tpm_type)) {
 		dev_err(dev, "unsupported TPM type %#llx (%s)\n",
 			tpm_type, qcom_tpm_type_name(tpm_type));
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
+	dev_info(dev, "using %s TPM backend\n", qcom_tpm_type_name(tpm_type));
+
 	ret = qcom_tpm_qsee_query_info(qtpm);
 	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to query TPM app info\n");
